WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
At a plague cemetery in London,
archaeologists found one hundred and

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.762
eighty-one coins. They lay in the grave of
a woman who died during the epidemic.

00:00:08.762 --> 00:00:11.244
We do not know why the money remained with
her.

00:00:11.352 --> 00:00:15.048
Perhaps people feared infection if they
removed the dead woman's clothing.

00:00:15.048 --> 00:00:19.048
But the same cemetery holds another
important detail: even in the communal

00:00:19.048 --> 00:00:22.139
graves, bodies were carefully laid out in
rows.

00:00:22.171 --> 00:00:25.143
Fear did not bring all essential work to a
stop.

00:00:25.143 --> 00:00:27.124
Someone continued to do it.

00:00:27.124 --> 00:00:30.596
The cemetery was established near the
Tower of London when plague reached the

00:00:30.629 --> 00:00:32.762
city in late thirteen forty-eight.

00:00:32.762 --> 00:00:35.549
Ordinary burial grounds were running out
of space.

00:00:35.581 --> 00:00:37.943
People did not know what caused the
disease.

00:00:37.943 --> 00:00:41.295
They had to decide whether to open the
door to a neighbor, care for a sick

00:00:41.295 --> 00:00:43.352
relative, or go to market.

00:00:43.352 --> 00:00:47.886
Lives depended on those decisions, but
people often had no reliable way to tell

00:00:47.886 --> 00:00:51.701
which choice was right. How do you protect
yourself from a danger you cannot

00:00:51.771 --> 00:00:55.924
recognize? And how do you preserve a city
when everyone is afraid to come close to

00:00:55.924 --> 00:01:00.349
another person? Medieval Europe was not a
collection of isolated villages.

00:01:00.419 --> 00:01:03.091
Cities needed grain, cloth and metal.

00:01:03.162 --> 00:01:07.581
Ships sailed between ports, merchants
carried goods across borders, and people

00:01:07.581 --> 00:01:09.752
traveled for work and returned home.

00:01:09.752 --> 00:01:13.676
This network made life possible even in
places that could not produce everything

00:01:13.676 --> 00:01:17.219
they needed. It also created routes for
infection.

00:01:17.219 --> 00:01:20.559
Animals and parasites moved alongside
people and cargo.

00:01:20.559 --> 00:01:24.648
The disease could cross distances that no
single sick person traveled from beginning

00:01:24.648 --> 00:01:28.457
to end. By the end of thirteen
forty-seven, the epidemic was spreading

00:01:28.457 --> 00:01:32.648
across the Mediterranean. The following
year, it swept through Florence, reached

00:01:32.648 --> 00:01:34.883
England and made its way to London.

00:01:34.883 --> 00:01:38.857
The disaster also affected other parts of
Eurasia and North Africa.

00:01:38.857 --> 00:01:42.749
Looking at a map, it is tempting to draw a
single line and trace the disease's

00:01:42.857 --> 00:01:46.895
movement. In reality, there were many
routes and separate outbreaks.

00:01:46.895 --> 00:01:49.981
Neighboring regions could experience them
very differently.

00:01:49.981 --> 00:01:53.257
News of the disaster could arrive before
the disease itself.

00:01:53.257 --> 00:01:57.435
People in England knew of the approaching
danger before the first local outbreaks.

00:01:57.435 --> 00:02:01.949
But the warning did not answer the central
question: what should they do now?

00:02:01.981 --> 00:02:05.410
Behind every point on the map was a place
that still needed food.

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:07.314
An order could close the gates.

00:02:07.314 --> 00:02:10.057
An order alone could not feed the people
inside.

00:02:10.057 --> 00:02:15.053
So authorities faced a problem with no
simple solution: how to limit dangerous

00:02:15.238 --> 00:02:19.130
connections without depriving a city of
the things it needed to survive.

00:02:19.276 --> 00:02:23.053
For someone who fell ill, that vast
geography meant nothing.

00:02:23.162 --> 00:02:25.949
A fever began, followed by severe
weakness.

00:02:26.286 --> 00:02:30.819
In the bubonic form of plague, the lymph
nodes became painfully swollen, often in

00:02:30.819 --> 00:02:32.615
the groin or under the arms.

00:02:32.762 --> 00:02:35.815
Sometimes the infection affected the blood
or lungs.

00:02:35.962 --> 00:02:39.510
Today, a doctor knows what infection such
symptoms may indicate.

00:02:39.771 --> 00:02:44.076
In the fourteenth century, people looked
for different explanations: an imbalance

00:02:44.076 --> 00:02:47.891
within the body, corrupted air, or the
influence of heavenly bodies.

00:02:48.305 --> 00:02:51.472
Religious beliefs linked the disaster to
punishment for sin.

00:02:51.886 --> 00:02:56.000
If someone believes the air is dangerous,
fragrant herbs and incense seem like

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.114
reasonable protection. They act according
to the knowledge available to them.

00:03:00.114 --> 00:03:03.771
The problem is that this knowledge does
not explain what is happening.

00:03:03.771 --> 00:03:07.352
The cause was a bacterium: Yersinia
pestis.

00:03:07.352 --> 00:03:11.314
It persists in natural reservoirs among
small mammals and their fleas.

00:03:11.314 --> 00:03:15.168
A person can become infected through the
bite of an infected flea or through

00:03:15.238 --> 00:03:17.333
contact with infected animal tissue.

00:03:17.333 --> 00:03:21.187
In the pneumonic form, transmission
through respiratory droplets is possible

00:03:21.219 --> 00:03:23.505
during close contact with a sick person.

00:03:23.505 --> 00:03:27.390
Scientists are still investigating which
routes predominated in different medieval

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:31.505
outbreaks. They also debate the role of
parasites that live on humans.

00:03:31.505 --> 00:03:36.310
A single diagram featuring a rat cannot
confidently explain the entire pandemic.

00:03:36.381 --> 00:03:40.762
But people around someone who was ill saw
a simpler sequence: one person became

00:03:40.762 --> 00:03:43.930
sick, and then someone nearby became sick
too.

00:03:44.076 --> 00:03:48.310
Even without knowledge of bacteria,
closeness began to inspire fear.

00:03:48.419 --> 00:03:52.844
And so a question about disease became a
question about another person.

00:03:52.990 --> 00:03:55.853
When the sick person was a stranger, you
could step away.

00:03:55.962 --> 00:03:59.467
But what could you do when the person who
fell ill lived with you?

00:03:59.467 --> 00:04:02.857
In Florence, Giovanni Boccaccio described
this change.

00:04:02.857 --> 00:04:06.781
In the introduction to The Decameron, he
recounts how people tried to survive the

00:04:06.781 --> 00:04:11.530
epidemic. Authorities removed refuse,
barred sick people from entering the city,

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:13.714
and issued advice on staying healthy.

00:04:13.714 --> 00:04:17.110
Measures were taken, but the disaster
could not be stopped.

00:04:17.143 --> 00:04:20.876
Some limited their contact with others,
shut themselves away with a small circle

00:04:20.876 --> 00:04:23.314
of people and tried to live moderately.

00:04:23.314 --> 00:04:26.743
Others drank, visited taverns and sought
entertainment.

00:04:26.743 --> 00:04:28.343
Some left the city.

00:04:28.343 --> 00:04:32.381
This is a work of literature, not a census
of how every Florentine behaved.

00:04:32.381 --> 00:04:36.387
But it reveals the fear of people who no
longer knew which of their habits were

00:04:36.457 --> 00:04:40.577
safe. Particularly painful are the
passages about relatives who left the sick

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:44.952
without help. Boccaccio describes the
breaking of bonds people usually rely on

00:04:44.952 --> 00:04:49.295
in a crisis. A home, where a person should
have been cared for, could become a place

00:04:49.295 --> 00:04:52.419
of isolation. Not everyone had the means
to leave.

00:04:52.419 --> 00:04:56.272
Waiting out the disaster in the
countryside required money, shelter and

00:04:56.343 --> 00:04:58.324
people to provide for everyday life.

00:04:58.324 --> 00:05:02.438
In The Decameron itself, the group that
leaves Florence has those resources.

00:05:02.438 --> 00:05:05.524
But if everyone leaves, who will bring
water to the sick?

00:05:05.524 --> 00:05:08.343
Who will bake bread? Who will take away
the dead?

00:05:08.343 --> 00:05:11.314
This was one of the epidemic's hardest
contradictions.

00:05:11.314 --> 00:05:14.781
Staying away from the sick could reduce
the danger to an individual.

00:05:14.781 --> 00:05:18.552
Yet someone still had to care for those
who could no longer help themselves.

00:05:18.552 --> 00:05:22.438
And when we return to the London burials,
it becomes clear that a story in which

00:05:22.438 --> 00:05:24.539
everyone fled would be incomplete.

00:05:24.571 --> 00:05:28.610
At the East Smithfield cemetery there were
individual graves as well as communal

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:32.610
burial trenches. It is easy to imagine the
latter as chaotic pits.

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:35.505
But archaeologists found carefully
arranged bodies.

00:05:35.505 --> 00:05:38.177
The cemetery itself required organization.

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:43.162
Land had to be set aside, graves dug, the
dead brought in and burials carried out.

00:05:43.162 --> 00:05:46.667
The living did this work in a city where
they, too, could fall ill.

00:05:46.667 --> 00:05:49.371
Excavations cannot tell us what each of
them felt.

00:05:49.371 --> 00:05:53.752
But they show the result of their work:
even amid enormous losses, people were not

00:05:53.752 --> 00:05:55.962
always buried hastily and without order.

00:05:55.962 --> 00:05:59.015
At the same time, fear could lead to very
different actions.

00:05:59.415 --> 00:06:03.987
Across Europe, false accusations spread
against Jews: that they were poisoning

00:06:03.987 --> 00:06:05.935
wells and causing the disease.

00:06:06.044 --> 00:06:09.097
Long-standing prejudices found a new
justification.

00:06:09.168 --> 00:06:11.421
Persecution and mass killings followed.

00:06:11.682 --> 00:06:15.002
These accusations had nothing to do with
the true cause of the plague.

00:06:15.187 --> 00:06:19.155
People became victims of violence that
infection did not make inevitable.

00:06:19.225 --> 00:06:23.606
Other people committed it. So it would be
just as wrong to see the entire era only

00:06:23.606 --> 00:06:27.421
as a time of mutual help as it would be to
see nothing but cruelty.

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:30.730
Evidence of both care and brutality has
survived.

00:06:30.730 --> 00:06:34.539
That evidence matters no less than what we
know about the disease itself.

00:06:34.539 --> 00:06:38.577
Plague placed people in terrible
circumstances, but their actions did not

00:06:38.577 --> 00:06:40.482
all amount to the same response.

00:06:40.482 --> 00:06:44.673
By the early thirteen fifties, the first
wave had weakened in many places.

00:06:44.673 --> 00:06:47.301
Plague would return, again and again.

00:06:47.301 --> 00:06:51.574
But even now, survivors faced consequences
that the end of mass deaths could not

00:06:51.644 --> 00:06:56.025
undo. Historical accounts often cite
estimates that roughly a third, or as much

00:06:56.025 --> 00:06:58.615
as half, of Europe's population died.

00:06:58.615 --> 00:07:00.901
These are not the results of an exact
count.

00:07:00.901 --> 00:07:05.396
Medieval records are incomplete, and newer
research emphasizes how uneven the losses

00:07:05.396 --> 00:07:09.968
were. A percentage calculated for one city
cannot simply be applied to an entire

00:07:09.968 --> 00:07:14.088
continent. On the ground, however, the
consequences were tangible.

00:07:14.158 --> 00:07:18.273
Where many people had died, fields
remained with too few hands to work them,

00:07:18.273 --> 00:07:20.259
and workshops were short of workers.

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:22.692
Land alone did not produce a harvest.

00:07:22.692 --> 00:07:25.815
A workshop without a craftsperson could
not fill an order.

00:07:25.815 --> 00:07:29.206
Those who hired labor had to compete for
the people who remained.

00:07:29.206 --> 00:07:32.793
Some workers gained the opportunity to
demand higher wages or choose another

00:07:32.901 --> 00:07:36.901
employer. For landowners and workshop
owners, this meant higher costs and a

00:07:36.901 --> 00:07:40.177
weakening of their customary control over
other people's labor.

00:07:40.177 --> 00:07:44.145
In England, the response included an
ordinance in thirteen forty-nine and the

00:07:44.177 --> 00:07:46.768
Statute of Labourers, passed two years
later.

00:07:46.768 --> 00:07:50.774
They attempted to limit wages, workers'
movement and their ability to take better

00:07:50.806 --> 00:07:54.654
terms elsewhere. Able-bodied people were
required to accept work under the

00:07:54.654 --> 00:07:59.492
prescribed rules. Surviving did not yet
mean freedom to direct one's own life.

00:07:59.492 --> 00:08:03.454
New opportunities had to be fought for,
while those in power tried to restore the

00:08:03.454 --> 00:08:07.644
old relationships. The plague did not
abolish dependence and poverty in a single

00:08:07.644 --> 00:08:10.768
blow. Its consequences differed from place
to place.

00:08:10.768 --> 00:08:14.882
But the enormous losses forced people to
confront a question that had once seemed

00:08:14.882 --> 00:08:19.993
settled: what is human labor worth, and
who has the right to set its price?

00:08:20.139 --> 00:08:23.644
Ways of protecting a city from another
outbreak also changed.

00:08:23.644 --> 00:08:27.758
For a trading port, the challenge remained
the same: keep disease out without

00:08:27.758 --> 00:08:31.568
stopping a way of life that depended on
arriving people and cargo.

00:08:31.568 --> 00:08:36.412
In thirteen seventy-seven, the authorities
of Ragusa, today's Dubrovnik, adopted a

00:08:36.482 --> 00:08:40.368
special rule. People arriving from places
where the disease was raging were

00:08:40.368 --> 00:08:42.812
forbidden to enter the city immediately.

00:08:42.920 --> 00:08:47.415
First, they had to spend thirty days
apart, in designated places outside it.

00:08:47.415 --> 00:08:51.574
The rule also applied to local residents
returning from infected areas.

00:08:51.606 --> 00:08:55.307
Even someone with a home in the city could
be required to wait before going back

00:08:55.454 --> 00:08:58.812
inside. The wait itself was a severe
ordeal.

00:08:58.958 --> 00:09:02.730
Conditions in the first places of
isolation were harsh; food was brought in

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:06.844
from outside. The decision to separate
arrivals immediately created another

00:09:06.844 --> 00:09:09.739
obligation: to supply them with what they
needed.

00:09:09.739 --> 00:09:12.520
Visits to people in isolation were
restricted too.

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:15.650
The delivery of necessities had to be
arranged separately.

00:09:15.796 --> 00:09:19.225
Otherwise, the people coming to help would
themselves defeat the purpose of

00:09:19.225 --> 00:09:23.149
isolation. Later, a forty-day period
became widespread.

00:09:23.149 --> 00:09:26.621
The word quarantine comes from the Italian
word for forty.

00:09:26.730 --> 00:09:28.825
Doctors still could not see the pathogen.

00:09:28.825 --> 00:09:32.488
But the authorities were already changing
the way people came into contact with one

00:09:32.558 --> 00:09:34.679
another in an effort to reduce risk.

00:09:34.679 --> 00:09:38.311
They had found a measure they could carry
out even without a complete explanation of

00:09:38.311 --> 00:09:41.206
the disease. It was not a final victory.

00:09:41.206 --> 00:09:43.187
Plague returned for centuries.

00:09:43.187 --> 00:09:46.692
Nor did quarantine turn a port into an
impregnable fortress.

00:09:46.692 --> 00:09:50.768
But between helpless waiting and bringing
trade to a complete halt, another course

00:09:50.768 --> 00:09:54.501
of action had emerged: to separate a
person's arrival from their meeting with

00:09:54.501 --> 00:09:56.793
the city by a period of time.

00:09:56.825 --> 00:10:00.254
Now let us return to London, to the
cemetery near the Tower.

00:10:00.254 --> 00:10:03.606
The people buried there did not know what
had caused their illness.

00:10:03.606 --> 00:10:07.415
But something survived in their remains
that allowed researchers to investigate it

00:10:07.415 --> 00:10:11.073
centuries later: fragments of bacterial
DNA.

00:10:11.073 --> 00:10:15.073
In twenty eleven, researchers published a
reconstruction of the genome of the plague

00:10:15.073 --> 00:10:18.387
bacterium from the remains of victims of
the London epidemic.

00:10:18.387 --> 00:10:21.739
By then, Yersinia pestis had long been
known to science.

00:10:21.739 --> 00:10:25.473
Now researchers could connect it to
particular people who had died during the

00:10:25.473 --> 00:10:28.901
Black Death and study an ancient form of
the pathogen.

00:10:28.901 --> 00:10:32.863
Later, another important trace was found
far from Europe, in what is now

00:10:32.863 --> 00:10:37.130
Kyrgyzstan. In the Chüy Valley,
gravestones from thirteen thirty-eight and

00:10:37.130 --> 00:10:40.635
thirteen thirty-nine survived with
references to pestilence.

00:10:40.635 --> 00:10:44.254
Scientists studied remains from these
burials and found the plague bacterium

00:10:44.254 --> 00:10:48.711
there as well. The findings, published in
twenty twenty-two, placed the identified

00:10:48.711 --> 00:10:52.825
strain at the base of a genetic branching
associated with the Black Death.

00:10:52.825 --> 00:10:57.130
In other words, an important clue to the
early history of the pandemic had emerged,

00:10:57.130 --> 00:11:00.596
from about ten years before the disaster
in Florence and London.

00:11:00.596 --> 00:11:04.520
This is not a map of every infected
person's journey, nor does it establish

00:11:04.520 --> 00:11:08.673
who the first patient was. But later
theories can now be compared with physical

00:11:08.673 --> 00:11:10.316
traces of the disease itself.

00:11:10.501 --> 00:11:13.250
Genetic analysis can identify the
pathogen.

00:11:13.396 --> 00:11:17.815
To understand how people lived through the
disaster, we need other evidence too:

00:11:17.815 --> 00:11:21.364
letters, books, documents and the burials
themselves.

00:11:21.625 --> 00:11:24.793
The arrangement of the graves preserves
part of that story as well.

00:11:25.054 --> 00:11:27.078
The money remained with the dead woman.

00:11:27.111 --> 00:11:31.193
And beside her lay other people, buried in
an order that someone continued to

00:11:31.225 --> 00:11:34.806
maintain. We do not know the names of
those who dug the graves and brought the

00:11:34.806 --> 00:11:40.101
dead here. We do not know what drove them:
duty, wages, faith or care for their

00:11:40.101 --> 00:11:44.254
loved ones. But the city held together in
part because of their work.

00:11:44.254 --> 00:11:47.380
They carried it out without yet knowing
what was killing their neighbors.

